{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>וְהָיָה אִם לֹא תִשְׁמַע וְגו׳.</b> All these curses were spoken in singular language, whereas those in parashat Bechukotai were all spoken in plural language: ״וְאִם לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּ״ and so on — even though among those too there are many blows directed at the collective, such as: ״וְהָיוּ שָׁמֶיךָ אֲשֶׁר עַל רֹאשְׁךָ נְחֹשֶׁת,״ and likewise: ״יוֹלֵךְ ה׳ אוֹתְךָ וְאֶת מַלְכְּךָ״ and so on, and many besides. What is the reason that those in Torat Kohanim are forty-nine, doubled in number to ninety-eight here? Is this not a matter demanding explanation?" "And the reason for this is that those in Torat Kohanim were stated before the mutual guarantee that Israel accepted upon themselves in the oath at Mount Gerizim and Mount Ebal. Therefore they come in the number forty-nine, because fifty gates of understanding were created in the world and all were given to Moses minus one (Nedarim 38b). From this you learn that it is possible for the human species to enter the chamber of the palace of wisdom and understanding by means of forty-nine gates open before it — and they are the gates of righteousness, as it says: ״שַׁעֲרֵי צֶדֶק״ — into which the righteous enter to understand from within them understanding and knowledge of HaShem. But the sinner into whom a spirit of folly has entered, whose heart has grown coarse from understanding, sees forty-nine gates of understanding open before him and enters none of them — therefore he is stricken with the number forty-nine. And before the mutual guarantee, it is stated: ״וְאִם לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּ״ in plural language, to say specifically that if all of them together became defiled, only then are they worthy of those comprehensive blows, but the righteous person among them saves his own soul. But here, after the mutual guarantee, each person bears his own sin and the sin of his fellow. Therefore they come in the number ninety-eight, doubled, corresponding to his own sin and the sin of his fellow. And it is stated: ״וְהָיָה אִם לֹא תִשְׁמַע״ in singular language, with comprehensive blows, to tell you that even a single sinner brings curse upon the collective, as it is written (Deuteronomy 29:17–21): ״פֶּן יֵשׁ בָּכֶם אִישׁ אוֹ אִשָּׁה״ and so on, and it is written: ״וְרָאוּ אֶת מַכּוֹת הָאָרֶץ״ — hear from this that the many bear the sin of the individual, as it was in the episode of Achan (Joshua 7). Therefore it is stated: ״אִם לֹא תִשְׁמַע,״ that even an individual who does not listen causes blows to the many — and they are the comprehensive blows mentioned in the parashah." "Another matter: since it is stated: ״וְלֹא נָתַן ה׳ לָכֶם לֵב לָדַעַת״ and so on, ״עַד הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה,״ and it is written (Proverbs 2:3–4): ״כִּי אִם לַבִּינָה תִקְרָא לַתְּבוּנָה תִּתֵּן קוֹלֶךָ, אִם תְּבַקְשֶׁנָּה כַכָּסֶף וְכַמַּטְמוֹנִים תַּחְפְּשֶׂנָּה,״ and they expounded on this: why is it counted twice? — that is, forty-nine facets of purity and forty-nine facets of impurity (Jerusalem Talmud Sanhedrin chapter 4, halakhah 2). And a person cannot arrive at this investigation except through the understanding of the heart that grasps by means of forty-nine gates of understanding — for behold, it prefaced by saying ״כִּי אִם לַבִּינָה תִקְרָא,״ for understanding is double relative to wisdom, for the discerning person understands one thing from within another. And Scripture states that they did not have a heart to know — that is, understanding — until this day. Therefore, specifically on this day ninety-eight curses were appointed, for not having sought out the Torah doubly, forty-nine times. But those in Torat Kohanim were stated before they had become people of understanding to understand one thing from within another — that is, forty-nine from within forty-nine — therefore they came only in the number forty-nine, for they had only the capacity to understand the minimal primary intelligibles, which come in the number forty-nine at the very least."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:14:25.675737706Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}